-A simulation day is a working day (Monday to Friday) counted from the `sim init` date,
-without days marked with `sim dayoff`. One sprint is five such days.
+The simulation has its own clock. A real date becomes the next simulation day only when
+you work on it: the first `sim start` or `sim standup` of that date marks it. Days without
+work do not move the clock, so after a week away the simulation continues from the next
+day. The simulation calendar starts on the `sim init` date and has only working days
+(Monday to Friday), so a simulation date is never a weekend, even if you work on a real
+Saturday. One sprint is five simulation days.
+
+`sim standup` prints the simulation date as `Date` and the calendar date as `Real date`.
+`Hours today` counts the real date, and `This sprint` counts the current simulation sprint.
+The simulation days are listed in `$SIM_HOME/simdays`, one real date per line. The first
+run builds this file from the worklog. `sim dayoff` excludes a real date, also one that
+already started, and `sim init` starts the clock again.
 
 ## chaos
 
